            <ArticleTitle>Impact of a weight loss program on migraine in obese adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>394-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1468-1331.2012.03771.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Increased headache frequency and severity have been observed in obese populations, but the real impact of a weight loss treatment on headache has not been studied. We investigated this issue in a sample of obese adolescents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In all, 135 migraineurs, aged 14-18 years, with body mass index (BMI) ≥ 97 th percentile, participating in a 12-month-long program, were studied before and after treatment. The program included dietary education, specific physical training, and behavioral treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Decreases in weight (P &lt; 0.01), BMI (P &lt; 0.01), waist circumference (P &lt; 0.01), headache frequency (P &lt; 0.01) and intensity (P &lt; 0.01), use of acute medications (P &lt; 0.05), and disability (P &lt; 0.05) were observed at the end of the first 6-month period and were maintained through the second 6 months. Both lower baseline BMI and excess change in BMI were significantly associated with better migraine outcomes 12 months after the intervention program.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Significant improvements in both adiposity and headache data were observed in obese adolescents with migraine who participated in a 12-month-long interdisciplinary intervention program for weight loss. Initial body weight and amount of weight loss may be useful for clinicians to predict migraine outcomes.</AbstractText>
